@node     Bug Reporting, Authors, Mailing list, Overview
@comment  node-name,  next,  previous,  up
@section  Reporting bugs, sending suggestions or contribution
@cindex   bug
@cindex   bug report
@cindex   suggestions

  We welcome bug reports or suggestions for Heroes (both program or
documentation).  Please email them publicly (preferred) to
@email{heroes-discuss@@lists.sourceforge.net} or privately to
@email{duret_g@@epita.fr}.  For bug reports, please include enough
information to reproduce the problem.  Tipacaly:

@itemize @bullet
@item 
the version number of Heroes.

@item 
hardware, operating system, and compiler versions.

@item 
any unusual options you gave to `configure'.

@item 
the actions necessary to reproduce the bug.

@item 
a description of the problem (sometimes, a snapshot may be a 
good description).

@item 
anything else that you think would be helpful.

@end itemize

  When in doubt whether something is needed or not, include it.  It's
better to include too much than to leave out something important.

@cindex   coredump
  To get useful coredumps, @samp{./configure} with @samp{--enable-debug}
and @samp{--disable-sound}.

@cindex   patch
@cindex   diff
  Patches are most welcome; if possible, please make them with
@samp{diff -u} (@pxref{Unified Format,Unified Format,diff,diff.info,Diff
Manual}) or @samp{diff -c} (@pxref{Context Format,Context
Format,Diff,diff.info,Diff Manual}) and include @samp{ChangeLog} entries
(@pxref{Change Log,Change Log,emacs,emacs-e20,Emacs Manual}).

@cindex   shar
@cindex   GNU shar
  When sending email, please do not encode or split the messages in any
way if possible; it's much easier to deal with one plain text message,
however large, than many small ones.  GNU shar
(@uref{ftp://ftp.gnu.org/gnu/sharutils/}) is a convenient way of
packaging multiple and/or binary files for email.

@cindex   contributing
@cindex   TODO
  Do not hesitate to contribute.  This is YOUR game and GPL allow you to
do any modifications you want in the game (provided that you keep those
modifications under the GPL).  There are numerous places were you can
contribute: you can hack the code (if so, please read my TODO file),
design new levels (well, of course you will have to wait the port of the
editor to do that, unless you use the MS-DOS version), create new
drawings (e.g. new sets of tiles), track some new music or add your
personal touch here and there.  Yet, I will be quite reluctant do add
new sound-track because that tends to take a lot of place and would
augment a lot the size of the archive.

