@section configure options for Heroes
@cindex  configure options for Heroes

You may give several options to @samp{configure} to configure the
building of Heroes.

@table @samp
@item --disable-sound
@cindex --disable-sound
Do not link with LibMikMod, thus compiling Heroes without sound support.
Sound support is enabled by default, unless LibMikMod is not found.

@item --disable-joystick
@cindex --disable-joystick
Do not include joystick support in the game.  Joystick support is enabled
by default, unless you don't have GII or joystick support in SDL.

@item --with-sdl[=DIR]
@cindex --with-sdl
Specify that you want to link with SDL.  This is the default if LibGGI is
not installed.  If you installed SDL in some non-standard place, you can
specify the installation path as an argument.  For example:
@example
./configure --with-sdl=/home/adl/usr
@end example
@samp{configure} will then look for @file{/home/adl/usr/bin/sdl-config}
and configure Heroes accordingly.

@item --with-GGI[=DIR]
@cindex --with-GGI
Link with LibGGI.  This is the default if LibGGI is installed.
If you installed LibGGI in some non-standard place, you can
specify the installation path as an argument

@item --with-libmikmod-prefix[=DIR]
@cindex --with-libmikmod-prefix
Specify where LibMikMod is installed.

@item --enable-debug
@cindex --enable-debug
Turn on debugging options: compile and link with debugging symbols,
enable assertions, and debug code.  This is disabled by default because
it slow down the code and produce a bigger binary.

@item --enable-mem-debug
@cindex --enable-mem-debug
Turn on support for malloc debugging libraries such as electric fence or
dmalloc (dmalloc will be used if both are installed).  This is disable
by default.

@item --disable-user-config
@cindex --disable-user-config
Don't use a per-user configuration file, but a system wide file instead.
This configuration file record keyboard configuration, colors of
players, speed of the game, volume, etc. (everything that can be
configured from the OPTIONS menu of the game).  If you save this as a
global file, you will want to give special right to that file and to the
heroes binary so that it can read and write that file whoever ran it;
this could leads to security issue.

The default is to save those options in @file{~/.heroes/options.dat}, if
you specify this option they will be saved in
@file{DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/options.dat}.

@item --disable-user-scores
@cindex --disable-user-scores
Don't use a per-user scores file, but a system wide file instead.  If
you save this as a global file, you will want to give special right to
that file and to the heroes binary so that it can read and write that
file whoever ran it; this could leads to security issue.

The default is to save the scores in @file{~/.heroes/scores.dat}, if
you specify this option they will be saved in
@file{DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/scores.dat}.

@item --disable-user-saves
@cindex --disable-user-saves
Don't use a per-user saves file, but a system wide file instead.  This
file allow people to save their progression in Quest Mode.  Their is 10
save slots.  If you save this as a global file, you will want to give
special right to that file and to the heroes binary so that it can read
and write that file whoever ran it; this could leads to security issue.

The default is to save the games in @file{~/.heroes/savegame.dat}, if
you specify this option they will be saved in
@file{DATADIR/heroes/txt_cfg/savegame.dat}.

@end table

